eturns 'inconclusive' because the interval straddles zero.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Reference-trainer slope (seed 201 only, no interval) is -0.368 and lies inside the interval, 23% of the way from the lower to the upper endpoint; the evaluated-model point estimate is 0.348 larger.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.reference_delay_log_slope\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_log_slope\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Censored fraction is 0.40, i.e. 8 of the 20 primary runs (4 seeds x 5 widths).\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.censored_fraction\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Control maximum held-out accuracy is 0.012 against a ceiling of 0.10; missing episodes 0.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.control_max_val_acc\",\n    \"final.metric.missing_episodes\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Summary score (mean held-out-accuracy AUC over seeds and widths) is 0.729.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.combined_score\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Seed-level scores are 0.607, 0.733, 0.717, 0.860 (mean 0.729, sample s.d. 0.104); per-seed wall-clock seconds 2,468, 1,894, 2,062, 1,943 (seed 201 includes controls).\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n    \"final.metric.combined_score\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Derived: fitted multiplicative delay factor per width doubling is 0.980 (interval 0.557 to 1.425); over 16->256 it is 0.924 (interval 0.096 to 4.12); at the reference slope 0.230.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_log_slope\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_low\",\n    \"final.metric.grok_delay_slope_ci_high\",\n    \"final.metric.reference_delay_log_slope\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": []\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Derived: with delay floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps (ratio 40), OLS weights (-0.2,-0.1,0,0.1,0.2) over widths 16..256 give a maximum attainable |slope| of 0.3*ln(40)=1.107; a log-line\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Is Grokking Delay Width-Dependent? A Censoring-Aware Seed-Cluster Slope Test on Modular Addition Finds No Resolvable Trend\",\n  \"central_question\": \"In a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW on modular addition (p=97, train fraction 0.4, weight decay 1.0, full batch, 10,000-step limit), does the delay between fitting the training set and generalizing decrease with model width, as a pre-registered seed-cluster slope test can detect?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"Under a strictly specified and evaluator-owned protocol, the log-delay vs log2-width slope is -0.020 with a 95% seed-cluster bootstrap CI of [-0.585, 0.354] (4 seeds x 5 widths, 40% of runs censored at the step limit), so the 'delay decreases with width' prediction is neither supported nor refuted; the paper's contribution is a calibrated negative/inconclusive measurement, a censoring-aware test design whose resolution limits are made explicit, and a clear list of what the evidence cannot say.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The sealed final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.outcome, status completed, correct true), so a paper about the measured results is supported. But 'qualified' certifies that the evaluation ran validly; it does not say the width hypothesis was supported. The decision rule fixed by the task contract (CI entirely below zero = supported, at/above zero = refuted, straddling = inconclusive) yields 'inconclusive' because the CI straddles zero. That is the most defensible and most honest story: a measurement-resolution paper. Alternatives rejected: (a) capacity-crossover / critical-width story (P1-P3 of the first model variant) -- no t_mem, t_gen, width-by-fraction or d*(f) results exist in the grounded artifacts; (b) lazy-to-rich or effective-learning-rate confound explanation -- the hidden-layer reparametrization sweep script was written but never executed (no code executed","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
