{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C6WfKFMTr9q4rTr4Qeds5Y","type":"tool_result","content":"43:\\section{Introduction}\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n60:\\section{Related Work}\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\n64:\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization of training data has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}, and a method for estimating how much information a model stores about data points has been proposed and measured on language models \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n66:\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n70:\\section{Method}\n73:\\subsection{Problem setup and hypothesis}\n75:\\paragraph{Task.} Inputs are all $p^2=9{,}409$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\\in\\{0,\\dots,96\\}$ and label $(a+b)\\bmod 97$. For each seed, a random $40\\%$ of pairs ($3{,}763$) forms the training split and the remaining $5{,}646$ pairs are held out; the evaluator, not the model, owns the split, the checkpoint schedule, and all metrics.\n77:\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let $t_{\\mathrm{fit}}$ be the first checkpoint at which training accuracy is at least $0.99$, and $t_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ the first at which held-out accuracy is at least $0.99$. The delay is\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n85:\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.} Each seed fixes both the train/held-out split and the weight initialization, and the design has exactly one run per seed and width. Resampling seeds therefore propagates split and initialization variability jointly, and a seed is the unit of replication; run-to-run nondeterminism within a seed is not separated from it, and cannot be, since no seed is repeated at a width in the sealed design. Because the design is balanced, the pooled OLS slope equals the slope fitted to the width-wise means of $\\ln\\Delta$, and the bootstrap interval is the percentile range of that quantity over seed resamples.\n87:\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n103:\\subsection{Reference trainer, controls, and validity}\n105:\\paragraph{Reference trainer.} The evaluator also trains its own stock transformer with the same optimizer settings and no modifications (no initialization shrinkage, no multipliers, all positions processed), using the first final seed ($201$) at each of the five widths. The OLS slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ over these five single-seed delays is the reference slope. It has no interval.\n107:\\paragraph{Controls.} Two control runs at width $64$ and seed $201$ check that the measurement does not manufacture generalization: one trains on training labels that have been permuted (at least $90\\%$ of labels changed) and one performs zero optimizer steps. A control fails if its held-out accuracy ever exceeds $0.10$; chance level for $97$ classes is about $0.010$.\n109:\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call budgets were respected, the controls passed, and at least one run fit the training data to $0.99$. Only these three status fields are retained in the artifacts; per-condition diagnostics beyond them are not, so we treat any finer breakdown as unknown. Diagnostics reported by the evaluated model itself are untrusted and not used: every accuracy, delay, control, and statistic below is computed by the evaluator from returned predictions.\n111:\\subsection{Scope of the retained evidence}\n115:\\section{Results}\n118:\\subsection{Main result: the slope test is inconclusive}\n154:\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n192:\\subsection{Censoring}\n196:\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\n220:\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\n224:\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\n230:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n233:\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: memorization capacity (how early the training split is fitted), initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n235:\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings (Table~\\ref{tab:readings}). (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is more negative than the point estimate, but it is one seed without an interval and does not by itself support this reading. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n254:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), i.e.\\ runs that end below $0.99$ held-out accuracy are censored however close they come to it. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n256:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n278:\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n280:\\section{Conclusion}\n290:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n292:\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n323:\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n346:\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n374:\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n378:\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n382:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n      1 \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}\n      1 \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}\n      1 \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}\n      1 \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}\n      1 \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}\n      1 \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}\n      1 \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}\n      1 \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}\n      1 \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}\n      1 \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}\n      1 \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}\n      1 \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA}\n      1 \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO}\n      1 \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}\n      1 \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}\n      1 \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}\n      1 \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF}\n      1 \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB}\n      1 \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT}\n32","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2479a9e-e8d3-4dec-9fbb-cae951098cd8","uuid":"8ead62a1-387a-4a08-95bd-62a543a5d686","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:26:23.328Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"43:\\section{Introduction}\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n60:\\section{Related Work}\n62:\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} analyze small transformers trained on modular addition mechanistically and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\n64:\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization of training data has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}, and a method for estimating how much information a model stores about data points has been proposed and measured on language models \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n66:\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n70:\\section{Method}\n73:\\subsection{Problem setup and hypothesis}\n75:\\paragraph{Task.} Inputs are all $p^2=9{,}409$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\\in\\{0,\\dots,96\\}$ and label $(a+b)\\bmod 97$. For each seed, a random $40\\%$ of pairs ($3{,}763$) forms the training split and the remaining $5{,}646$ pairs are held out; the evaluator, not the model, owns the split, the checkpoint schedule, and all metrics.\n77:\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let $t_{\\mathrm{fit}}$ be the first checkpoint at which training accuracy is at least $0.99$, and $t_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ the first at which held-out accuracy is at least $0.99$. The delay is\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n85:\\paragraph{What the interval does and does not capture.} Each seed fixes both the train/held-out split and the weight initialization, and the design has exactly one run per seed and width. Resampling seeds therefore propagates split and initialization variability jointly, and a seed is the unit of replication; run-to-run nondeterminism within a seed is not separated from it, and cannot be, since no seed is repeated at a width in the sealed design. Because the design is balanced, the pooled OLS slope equals the slope fitted to the width-wise means of $\\ln\\Delta$, and the bootstrap interval is the percentile range of that quantity over seed resamples.\n87:\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n103:\\subsection{Reference trainer, controls, and validity}\n105:\\paragraph{Reference trainer.} The evaluator also trains its own stock transformer with the same optimizer settings and no modifications (no initialization shrinkage, no multipliers, all positions processed), using the first final seed ($201$) at each of the five widths. The OLS slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ over these five single-seed delays is the reference slope. It has no interval.\n107:\\paragraph{Controls.} Two control runs at width $64$ and seed $201$ check that the measurement does not manufacture generalization: one trains on training labels that have been permuted (at least $90\\%$ of labels changed) and one performs zero optimizer steps. A control fails if its held-out accuracy ever exceeds $0.10$; chance level for $97$ classes is about $0.010$.\n109:\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call budgets were respected, the controls passed, and at least one run fit the training data to $0.99$. Only these three status fields are retained in the artifacts; per-condition diagnostics beyond them are not, so we treat any finer breakdown as unknown. Diagnostics reported by the evaluated model itself are untrusted and not used: every accuracy, delay, control, and statistic below is computed by the evaluator from returned predictions.\n111:\\subsection{Scope of the retained evidence}\n115:\\section{Results}\n118:\\subsection{Main result: the slope test is inconclusive}\n154:\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n192:\\subsection{Censoring}\n196:\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\n220:\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\n224:\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\n230:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n233:\\paragraph{Relation to the explanations of grokking.} Each account in Section~\\ref{sec:related} associates the delay with a quantity that can depend on width or parametrization: memorization capacity (how early the training split is fitted), initial weight norm and the loss landscape, the relative efficiency of memorizing and generalizing circuits, and the position on the lazy-to-rich axis. The evaluated model touches the last two knobs directly: its initial weights are shrunk by half, which the source comments describe as aimed at reducing lazy-regime memorization, and its multipliers change how large a step each layer takes at a given width. A clear width slope from this model could therefore reflect the multipliers rather than width alone, and a flat slope could reflect their compensating a stock-model dependence. This is why the isolating comparison with a matched stock model (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}, item iv) matters more than a further estimate of the slope for the evaluated model alone.\n235:\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings (Table~\\ref{tab:readings}). (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is more negative than the point estimate, but it is one seed without an interval and does not by itself support this reading. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n254:\\paragraph{Threshold and time-grid sensitivity.} The delay is read off a $250$-step grid with a $0.99$ threshold on both accuracies, so it is blind to differences below one probe interval and sensitive to where a run plateaus. The development evaluation shows how this can matter: all six runs of one configuration were censored even though their mean final held-out accuracy was $0.944$ (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}), i.e.\\ runs that end below $0.99$ held-out accuracy are censored however close they come to it. We did not vary the threshold or the grid in the sealed evaluation, so we cannot say how the slope would change under, for example, a $0.95$ threshold, and the retained artifacts do not allow it to be recomputed.\n256:\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n278:\\paragraph{Limitations.} Table~\\ref{tab:claims} summarizes which claims the evidence does and does not support. The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n280:\\section{Conclusion}\n290:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n292:\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n323:\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n346:\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n374:\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n378:\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n382:\\subsection{LLM usage}\n      1 \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}\n      1 \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}\n      1 \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}\n      1 \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}\n      1 \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}\n      1 \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}\n      1 \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}\n      1 \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}\n      1 \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO}\n      1 \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}\n      1 \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}\n      1 \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA}\n      1 \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO}\n      1 \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}\n      1 \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}\n      1 \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}\n      1 \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF}\n      1 \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB}\n      1 \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT}\n32","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
