{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PWE9SEKkGZWodZS2hfKndQ","type":"tool_result","content":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n\nThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n\nThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n\\label{sec:model}\nThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. Writing $z_i=E[x_i]+P_i$ for the embedded tokens $x_1,x_2,x_3{=}$``$=$'', the evaluated model computes\n\\begin{equation}\nh=z_3+W_O\\,\\mathrm{Attn}(z_1,z_2,z_3),\\qquad h'=h+W_2\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1h+b_1)+b_2,\\qquad \\text{logits}=m_d\\,W_U\\,h',\n\\end{equation}\nwith $W_\\ast=m_dV_\\ast$ for the attention and MLP matrices (Modification 3 below; $m_d=1$ recovers the plain parametrization) and $W_U$ the unembedding. The parameter count is $12d^2+(2p+9)d$, from $6{,}320$ at $d=16$ to $838{,}400$ at $d=256$, within the $10^6$ budget (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-model}).\n\nRelative to the evaluator's stock model, the configuration has three modifications, all visible in the source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_model/program.py}):\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\emph{Smaller initial weights.} All hidden and output weight matrices are multiplied by $0.5$ at initialization.\n\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n\n28:\\title{Does Grokking Delay Depend on Width? An Inconclusive, Censoring-Aware Slope Test on Modular Addition}\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n38:The measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome \n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n79:\\Delta = \\begin{cases} \\max(t_{\\mathrm{gen}}-t_{\\mathrm{fit}},\\,250) & \\text{if both thresholds are reached,}\\\\ 10{,}000 & \\text{otherwise (censored).}\\end{cases}\n81:A run is censored if \\emph{either} threshold is never reached. Censored runs keep the cap value and are never dropped or reweighted. The floor of one probe interval ($250$ steps) also applies when held-out accuracy crosses $0.99$ no later than training accu\n113:The retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-w\n138:Censored fraction of the 20 runs & $0.40$ ($8$ runs) \\\\\n192:\\subsection{Censoring}\n193:Forty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real depende\n194:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $1\n47:This paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, a\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Sec\n56:\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s\n121:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusi\n135:95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\n282:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. T","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2479a9e-e8d3-4dec-9fbb-cae951098cd8","uuid":"a4d9bff6-4529-4726-9b94-6f43996dfa43","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:26:25.480Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n\nThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n\nThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n\\label{sec:model}\nThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. Writing $z_i=E[x_i]+P_i$ for the embedded tokens $x_1,x_2,x_3{=}$``$=$'', the evaluated model computes\n\\begin{equation}\nh=z_3+W_O\\,\\mathrm{Attn}(z_1,z_2,z_3),\\qquad h'=h+W_2\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1h+b_1)+b_2,\\qquad \\text{logits}=m_d\\,W_U\\,h',\n\\end{equation}\nwith $W_\\ast=m_dV_\\ast$ for the attention and MLP matrices (Modification 3 below; $m_d=1$ recovers the plain parametrization) and $W_U$ the unembedding. The parameter count is $12d^2+(2p+9)d$, from $6{,}320$ at $d=16$ to $838{,}400$ at $d=256$, within the $10^6$ budget (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-model}).\n\nRelative to the evaluator's stock model, the configuration has three modifications, all visible in the source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_model/program.py}):\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\emph{Smaller initial weights.} All hidden and output weight matrices are multiplied by $0.5$ at initialization.\n\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n\n28:\\title{Does Grokking Delay Depend on Width? An Inconclusive, Censoring-Aware Slope Test on Modular Addition}\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\n38:The measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about two-fifths of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome \n68:\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n79:\\Delta = \\begin{cases} \\max(t_{\\mathrm{gen}}-t_{\\mathrm{fit}},\\,250) & \\text{if both thresholds are reached,}\\\\ 10{,}000 & \\text{otherwise (censored).}\\end{cases}\n81:A run is censored if \\emph{either} threshold is never reached. Censored runs keep the cap value and are never dropped or reweighted. The floor of one probe interval ($250$ steps) also applies when held-out accuracy crosses $0.99$ no later than training accu\n113:The retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-w\n138:Censored fraction of the 20 runs & $0.40$ ($8$ runs) \\\\\n192:\\subsection{Censoring}\n193:Forty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real depende\n194:% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n37:Delay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $1\n47:This paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, a\n51:\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Sec\n56:\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one\n83:\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s\n121:The slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusi\n135:95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\n282:We tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. T","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
